### Operational Experience: World Cup & Live Event Delivery

* **Totalplay (Mexico)**
  * Jose Luis Rodriguez Lopez presented [Streaming the World Cup at Totalplay](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-mops-streaming-the-world-cup-at-totalplay-00).
  * Totalplay operates a widespread optical network across Mexico comprising ~20 distinct market topologies.
  * Exclusivity rights for live sports (such as FIFA World Cup matches on VIX or major boxing matches like Canelo vs. Crawford) drive massive, localized traffic spikes. The Canelo vs. Crawford match generated a peak 5x larger than any previously observed peak.
  * Totalplay relies on IP Multicast for linear TV channels to set-top boxes, combined with unicast edge caching. Controlled dry-runs with content providers helped test header overflows and partial cache outage responses.

* **OpenX (Brazil)**
  * Renato Ornelas presented [When the World Cup Moved to YouTube: 416 Gbps and the Limits of Local Delivery in Brazil](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-mops-when-the-world-cup-moved-to-youtube-416-gbps-and-the-limits-of-local-delivery-in-brazil-00).
  * OpenX serves regional ISPs in Brazil. Digital rights for the FIFA World Cup were held by Live Mode / CazéTV on YouTube, producing 29 of the 30 largest YouTube live streams in history.
  * Local Google cache capacity (79 Gbps) and local PNIs (200 Gbps) were fully saturated, causing traffic to overflow from international locations (Miami, Europe) over transit links to reach Brazilian end-users.
  * Despite traffic taking international paths, end-user experience was maintained. Renato requested IETF standardization around CDN-to-ISP signaling for traffic steering and preserving flow-level visibility for network operators.

### Industry Panel: Live Video Delivery Challenges in the Americas
* **Panelists**: Glenn Deen, Sanjay Mishra, Jose Luis Rodriguez Lopez, Renato Ornelas. **Moderator**: Jenna Inger.
* **Scaling and Peak Management**:
  * Live events produce extreme peak-to-average traffic ratios. Building core origin backhaul ("highways") for infrequent peak events is not cost-effective; the trend is deploying smaller distributed edge caches ("parking lots").
  * Linear sports streams are transitioning rapidly to IP/OTT. Unlike file/game downloads, live sports streams are completely unforgiving to buffering or latency issues.
* **Protocol Evolution, QUIC, and Telemetry**:
  * Encryption in QUIC, Media over QUIC (MoQ), and overlays blinds operators to flow characteristics, making troubleshooting QoE degradation or network congestion difficult.
  * Panelists called for standardized cross-layer telemetry and operational visibility hooks without compromising user privacy. Sanjay Mishra noted that the SCONE Working Group is addressing network-application metadata exchanges.
* **Legacy Clients and Deployment Realities**:
  * Millions of deployed smart TVs and set-top boxes remain un-updated for a decade or more and do not support HTTP/3, QUIC, or native IP multicast.
  * To bridge this gap, operators use Multicast-ABR delivered to Customer Premises Equipment (CPE / ONTs) acting as "Nano CDNs," which translate multicast streams into local unicast HTTP fetches for legacy home devices (a concept also discussed by Kyle Rose and Tom Hill in chat).
* **Microphone Discussion**:
  * *Rich Martin*: Asked if streaming architecture will shift from pull-based document caching to publish-subscribe paradigms. Panelists noted that while PubSub models (like MoQ) offer benefits, the massive footprint of legacy HTTP-based client devices requires long-term backward compatibility.
  * *T.Y.*: Inquired about multicast deployment hurdles. Jose Luis Rodriguez Lopez and Glenn Deen noted that while internal core multicast operates reliably, lack of client-side device support and price sensitivity among TV manufacturers limit native end-to-end multicast.
  * *Nalini Elkins*: Highlighted the tension between encryption and operational management, suggesting future work on privacy-preserving telemetry architectures and client proxies.

